<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haakon VI of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1340">1340</a>–<a href="/years/1380">1380</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haakon VI was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1343 to 1380 and also king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1362 to 1364. He married Margaret, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a>, in 1363, and their son Olaf inherited both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Norway. After the deaths of Haakon and Olaf, Margaret united the three Nordic crowns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haakon-vi-of-norway.jpg" alt="Haakon VI of Norway" width="287" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawing by Hakon Thorsen (d. before 1924) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kong_Haakon_VI_Magnusson_PI_XVII_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haakon was the younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-eriksson">Magnus Eriksson</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and Blanche of Namur, and was born in 1340, most likely in Sweden. He belonged to the Swedish House of Bjälbo and, through his great-grandfather Haakon V, was considered an acceptable heir by the Norwegian nobility. He was raised in Norway to prepare him to rule there. On 15 August 1343 letters were issued stating that the king and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a> had decided to place him on the Norwegian throne, and in the following year representatives of the cities and the public hailed him as king at Båhus Castle. Magnus abdicated the Norwegian throne in August 1355, though he kept control of parts of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Haakon's elder brother Eric rebelled against their father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and when Eric died of the plague in 1359 the Swedish nobility deposed Magnus and elected Haakon king of Sweden at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> on 15 February 1362. In 1363 Haakon married Margaret, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a>, at Copenhagen Cathedral. That same year Swedish nobles led by Bo Jonsson Grip persuaded Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg">Albert II of Mecklenburg</a> to depose Magnus and Haakon in favour of his son Albert, who was crowned in 1364. Haakon and Magnus lost most of Sweden at the battle of Gataskogen, where Magnus was captured and held for six years.</p><p>In 1371 Haakon's campaign ended in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> and a peace treaty signed on 14 August 1371, which freed Magnus for a ransom of 12,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks, more than the kingdom's ordinary tax income before the plague, so that a special tax was imposed.</p><p>Haakon allied with Valdemar, turning Norwegian policy eastwards. In 1367 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-cologne">Confederation of Cologne</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and raided the Norwegian coast, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> of 24 May 1370 ended the war and left the League with a virtual monopoly on trade. When Valdemar died on 24 October 1375, Haakon borrowed money for the League's neutrality, and on 3 May 1376 his son Olav was elected king of Denmark at Slagelse. In 1379 Haakon confirmed Henry Sinclair as Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1349 and killed between 50 and 60 per cent of its population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Haakon died on 11 September 1380 and was succeeded by Olav, with Margaret as regent. Olav died childless seven years later, and Margaret asserted authority over all three Scandinavian kingdoms as their first female monarch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haakon VI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Black Death of 1349 cut royal and church revenues sharply, and in 1380 the death of <b>Haakon VI</b> left the crown to his son Olaf, already king of Denmark, beginning more than four centuries of union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Margaret I, daughter of Valdemar IV of Denmark and widow of <b>Haakon VI of Norway</b>, became ruler of Denmark and Norway and, after defeating King Albert of Mecklenburg in 1389, of Sweden too.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=61,15&amp;year=1366" aria-label="Sweden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Haakon VI of Norway"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4385 4844h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4385 4844h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=61,15&amp;year=1366">What was here in 1366? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
